CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 2 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
`*d = 8`, with `d` pointing to `dA` or `dB` and the matching mutex held,
is lowered to one guarded assignment per target:

    dA#3 == (d == &dA ? 8 : dA#2)
    dB#3 == (d == &dA ? dB#2 : 8)

The write events were already conditioned on the target. Renaming the old
value (`dA#2`, `dB#2`) still recorded a shared READ of it, carrying only the
path guard. So when `d == &dB`, main was seen reading `dA` while holding
`mB`, and that read was paired with the thread's write of `dA` under `mA`:
a race that no execution has. The program reads neither old value; it is
the lowering framing the untouched target back over itself.

Reported FAILED before the fix, SUCCESSFUL after. Reduced from
goblint-regression/06-symbeq_11-equ_nr, which the fix also corrects (so does
06-symbeq_13-equ_proc_nr).

See `store_through_pointer_framing_race`: the same program with the locks
swapped really races, and must stay FAILED.
